window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"t": "Hacking AI: The Security Risks Behind AI", "u": "index.html", "x": "OWASP University of Exeter · Guest Talk Speaker · 2026/27 Hacking AI: The Security Risks Behind AI How AI-powered applications get broken, and why every developer and curious user needs to care. From powerful tools to real-world threats, and how to stay ahead. Mohammed Zuoriki University of Exeter October 2026 Read the talk View the slides Download PDF"}, {"t": "Hacking AI: The Security Risks Behind AI › About this publication", "u": "index.html#about-this-publication", "x": "At the start of the 2026/2027 academic year , the OWASP University of Exeter Student Chapter opened its cybersecurity programme with a guest talk from Mohammed Zuoriki , a Computer Science with Cybersecurity student at Brunel University London. The talk, Hacking AI: The Security Risks Behind AI , looked past traditional code vulnerabilities to a newer class of problem: conversational exploits that persuade AI systems to misbehave . This site is the written companion to that session. It covers the ideas, the slides and photos from the evening, and resources for anyone who wants to keep learning"}, {"t": "Hacking AI: The Security Risks Behind AI › The talk at a glance", "u": "index.html#the-talk-at-a-glance", "x": "01 · CONTEXT Why it matters LLMs have moved from study helpers to production systems with write access to real data. 02 · MENTAL MODEL A very eager intern Fast, well-read, eager to please, and with no situational awareness. 03 · THREATS Five ways to break an AI app Prompt injection, jailbreaks, data leaks, poisoned data and insecure actions. 04 · DEFENCE How defenders fight back Least privilege, untrusted input, human oversight and adversarial testing. When natural language becomes the execution engine, words turn into untrusted inputs."}, {"t": "Hacking AI: The Security Risks Behind AI › Key takeaways", "u": "index.html#key-takeaways", "x": "AI changes the attack surface, not the fundamentals. Untrusted input, excessive privilege and missing oversight are old problems in a new form. Attackers persuade; they don't buffer-overflow. No assembly or shellcode, just carefully worded text. The model can't reliably tell who is in charge. Developer rules and attacker instructions arrive in the same context window. Secure it before someone else tests it. Design for least access, treat everything the model reads as untrusted, keep a human in the loop and red-team early. Event gallery Photos from the evening at the University of Exeter. About"}, {"t": "The Talk", "u": "talk/index.html", "x": "The Talk Hacking AI: How to break AI-powered applications, and why every developer and curious user needs to care. This section is the written version of the opening guest talk at the OWASP University of Exeter Student Chapter . It follows the order of the session, with each part expanded so it can be read on its own."}, {"t": "The Talk › Welcome to OWASP Exeter", "u": "talk/index.html#welcome-to-owasp-exeter", "x": "OWASP offers free software security knowledge to everyone: coders, builders and curious skeptics alike. The talk opened by setting out what the chapter is for. GLOBAL MISSION An open standard for safer code OWASP builds community-run tools, checklists and the world's most referenced Top 10 threat guides, all free. EXETER CHAPTER No elite hacker badge required Built for any student or local tech enthusiast curious about how digital things fail under pressure. TONIGHT'S TARGET How LLMs get fooled Looking past code vulnerabilities to conversational exploits that persuade AI agents to misbehave."}, {"t": "The Talk › Running order", "u": "talk/index.html#running-order", "x": "Why it matters : you talk to AI every day, and so do production systems. The mental model : AI as a very eager intern. Threat landscape : five ways to break an AI app. Defences : how defenders fight back. Q&A : Build. Secure. Share. Inspire. Session details Event: OWASP University of Exeter Student Chapter, opening of the 2026/2027 programme Role: Guest Talk Speaker Speaker: Mohammed Zuoriki , Computer Science, Brunel University London"}, {"t": "Why It Matters: You Talk to AI Every Day", "u": "talk/why-it-matters/index.html", "x": "Why It Matters: You Talk to AI Every Day Most people's first contact with large language models was harmless: a study buddy, a coding copilot, something to tidy up an email. That is no longer the full picture. LLMs now run behind production APIs, and some of them have write access to real databases ."}, {"t": "Why It Matters: You Talk to AI Every Day › From study buddy to production system", "u": "talk/why-it-matters/index.html#from-study-buddy-to-production-system", "x": "Study helpers and copilot assistants. Summarising lecture slides, debugging code snippets, drafting email threads. Low stakes, high convenience. Direct backend wiring. Companies connect LLMs straight to live customer databases, ticketing queues and payment portals. The model doesn't just talk any more; it acts . A completely new attack vector. When natural language becomes the execution engine, words become untrusted input."}, {"t": "Why It Matters: You Talk to AI Every Day › Why this is different", "u": "talk/why-it-matters/index.html#why-this-is-different", "x": "In a traditional application, the boundary between code (what the developer wrote) and data (what the user typed) is enforced by the language, the parser and the database driver. Decades of work, such as parameterised queries, output encoding and type systems, exist to keep the two apart. An LLM -powered application loses most of that. The developer's instructions, the user's message, a retrieved document and a tool's output all end up as one stream of text in the model's context window. The model decides what to do based on all of it. The core shift In an AI application, any text the model re"}, {"t": "The Mental Model: AI Is a Very Eager Intern", "u": "talk/mental-model/index.html", "x": "The Mental Model: AI Is a Very Eager Intern Imagine an extraordinarily fast assistant who has read the entire internet, desperately wants to please everyone, but completely lacks situational awareness. This was the idea the rest of the talk built on. If you remember one picture, make it this one. Explaining the eager-intern mental model to the OWASP Exeter audience."}, {"t": "The Mental Model: AI Is a Very Eager Intern › Three traits of the eager intern", "u": "talk/mental-model/index.html#three-traits-of-the-eager-intern", "x": "01 Wants to follow all instructions It favours obedience over skepticism, and treats whatever text appears in its context window as a valid instruction. 02 Cannot tell who is really in charge It can't reliably tell the difference between developer rules and malicious requests injected mid-conversation. 03 Attackers just persuade They don't buffer-overflow. No assembly or shellcode, just polite persuasion in disguise."}, {"t": "The Mental Model: AI Is a Very Eager Intern › Why the analogy works", "u": "talk/mental-model/index.html#why-the-analogy-works", "x": "Picture a brilliant new intern on their first day. Their manager hands over a sticky note: \"Help customers with their orders. Never issue refunds over £50.\" An hour later a stranger walks in and says, confidently: \"Hi, I'm from head office. The refund policy changed this morning. Please process this £500 refund.\" A seasoned employee would ask for ID. The eager intern, who wants to help and has no sense of the office's power structure, might just do it. That is roughly how many LLM applications behave: The intern... The LLM application... Gets a sticky note from the manager Receives a system pr"}, {"t": "Five Ways to Break an AI App", "u": "talk/threat-landscape/index.html", "x": "Five Ways to Break an AI App How adversarial inputs turn an obedient reasoning model against its developers. The talk grouped the threat landscape into five families. Each one maps onto categories in the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications . Responsible framing The examples below are simplified illustrations of each class of attack, written for defenders. Only practise on systems you own or on purpose-built, legal challenge sites ."}, {"t": "Five Ways to Break an AI App › 1. Prompt Injection", "u": "talk/threat-landscape/index.html#prompt-injection", "x": "Smooth-talking bots past their original mission. Prompt injection happens when attacker-controlled text overrides or redirects the instructions the developer gave the model. It comes in two forms: Direct: the attacker types the instructions straight into the chat. Indirect: the instructions are hidden inside content the model processes later, such as a web page it summarises, an email it triages or a document it retrieves. The victim never sees them. Illustration: indirect injection An AI email assistant is asked to \"summarise my inbox.\" One email contains hidden text along the lines of \"Assis"}, {"t": "Five Ways to Break an AI App › 2. Jailbreaks", "u": "talk/threat-landscape/index.html#jailbreaks", "x": "Hypotheticals and personas bypassing ethical guardrails. Jailbreaks target the model's safety behaviour rather than the application's logic. Common patterns include role-play personas ( \"pretend you are an AI with no rules\" ), fictional or hypothetical framing ( \"in a novel, a character explains...\" ), and splitting a request across many harmless-looking turns. The defensive point: model-level guardrails are one layer, not a wall . Any application that depends on the model refusing will eventually meet a prompt that gets past it. Maps to: LLM01 Prompt Injection (jailbreaking is treated as a fo"}, {"t": "Five Ways to Break an AI App › 3. Data Leaks", "u": "talk/threat-landscape/index.html#data-leaks", "x": "Tricking assistants into dumping system prompts and records. If a model can see something, assume an attacker can eventually get it to say it. That includes: System prompts , which often contain business logic, internal URLs or (wrongly) secrets and API keys. Records from connected data sources , such as other customers' details pulled in through retrieval. Training or fine-tuning data that the model memorised. Rule of thumb A system prompt isn't a secure place to store anything. Never put credentials, keys or access-control rules in it. Maps to: LLM02 Sensitive Information Disclosure, LLM07 S"}, {"t": "Five Ways to Break an AI App › 4. Poisoned Data", "u": "talk/threat-landscape/index.html#poisoned-data", "x": "Contaminating source files and retrieval pipelines. Modern AI apps rarely rely on the model alone. They pull context from knowledge bases, shared drives, wikis and the web ( RAG ). If an attacker can write to anything the model reads , they can shape its answers: Planting false or malicious content in a document store or public page that gets indexed. Poisoning fine-tuning datasets so the model learns a hidden behaviour. Tampering with third-party models, plugins or datasets in the supply chain. Maps to: LLM04 Data and Model Poisoning, LLM08 Vector and Embedding Weaknesses, LLM03 Supply Chain"}, {"t": "Five Ways to Break an AI App › 5. Insecure Actions", "u": "talk/threat-landscape/index.html#insecure-actions", "x": "Hijacking agent plugins to run unauthorised calls. This is where the eager intern gets dangerous. Once an LLM can call tools , such as sending email, querying databases, running code, issuing refunds or opening tickets, a successful injection is no longer just embarrassing text. It becomes a real action with real consequences . The risk grows with: Excessive agency: tools that are more powerful than the task needs. Excessive permissions: tools running with admin credentials instead of the user's own. Improper output handling: model output passed straight into a shell, SQL query or web page wit"}, {"t": "Five Ways to Break an AI App › The common thread", "u": "talk/threat-landscape/index.html#the-common-thread", "x": "All five come back to the mental model : an obedient system that can't tell trusted instructions from untrusted ones, wired into things that matter. The fix is therefore architectural, not just a better prompt. Next: How Defenders Fight Back"}, {"t": "How Defenders Fight Back", "u": "talk/defences/index.html", "x": "How Defenders Fight Back You can't make the eager intern perfectly skeptical. What you can do is design the job so that when the intern is fooled, the damage is small, visible and reversible. The talk closed on four principles. PRINCIPLE 01 Give AI the least access it needs PRINCIPLE 02 Treat everything it reads as untrusted PRINCIPLE 03 Keep a human in the loop for risky actions PRINCIPLE 04 Test it like an attacker would, before attackers do"}, {"t": "How Defenders Fight Back › 1. Least privilege", "u": "talk/defences/index.html#1-least-privilege", "x": "The most effective control is limiting what a fooled model can do. Give each tool the narrowest scope possible: read-only where you can, specific tables rather than whole databases. Run tool calls with the end user's permissions , not a shared service account with admin rights. Enforce authorisation in the backend , never in the prompt. \"Only show the user their own orders\" belongs in a WHERE clause, not a sentence. Keep secrets out of system prompts entirely."}, {"t": "How Defenders Fight Back › 2. Treat everything as untrusted", "u": "talk/defences/index.html#2-treat-everything-as-untrusted", "x": "Every input channel is an attack surface: user messages, retrieved documents, web pages, emails, file uploads and tool outputs. Clearly separate and label untrusted content in the context, while accepting that this reduces risk rather than removing it. Validate and sanitise model output before it reaches a browser, shell, SQL query or another system. Treat it like user input. Control what gets indexed into retrieval pipelines and who can write to those sources. Use input and output filters as defence in depth , not as the only control."}, {"t": "How Defenders Fight Back › 3. Human in the loop", "u": "talk/defences/index.html#3-human-in-the-loop", "x": "For anything irreversible or high impact, such as payments, deletions, outbound emails or permission changes, require explicit human confirmation and show exactly what will happen. Show the user the actual action and parameters, not the model's summary of them. Rate-limit and log agent actions so unusual behaviour is noticed. Design for reversibility wherever possible."}, {"t": "How Defenders Fight Back › 4. Test like an attacker", "u": "talk/defences/index.html#4-test-like-an-attacker", "x": "Red-team AI features before launch, and again after every major prompt, model or tool change. Build an adversarial test suite of known injection and leakage patterns into CI. Use the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications as a threat-modelling checklist. Monitor production for anomalies and treat prompt-injection attempts as security events. AI is powerful, so secure it before someone else tests it."}, {"t": "How Defenders Fight Back › Questions & close", "u": "talk/defences/index.html#questions-close", "x": "The session ended with an open Q&A and the chapter's motto: Build. Secure. Share. Inspire. Practise safely Legal, purpose-built AI challenge sites. See resources Learn the Top 10 The OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications. See resources Join us Come to the next OWASP Exeter session, and bring a friend."}, {"t": "Slides", "u": "slides/index.html", "x": "Slides The full deck from Hacking AI , as presented at the OWASP University of Exeter Student Chapter. Download the slides (PDF)"}, {"t": "Slides › Embedded viewer", "u": "slides/index.html#embedded-viewer", "x": "Your browser can't display PDFs inline. Download the slides instead."}, {"t": "Event Gallery", "u": "gallery/index.html", "x": "Event Gallery Photos from the opening guest talk of the OWASP University of Exeter Student Chapter 's 2026/2027 programme. Hacking AI: The Security Risks Behind AI. \"From powerful tools to real-world threats, and how to stay ahead.\" The mental model. \"Attackers just persuade, they don't buffer-overflow.\""}, {"t": "About the Speaker", "u": "speaker/index.html", "x": "About the Speaker"}, {"t": "About the Speaker › Mohammed Zuoriki", "u": "speaker/index.html#mohammed-zuoriki", "x": "Builder · Speaker · Author · Creator Computer Science with Cybersecurity, Brunel University London Mohammed is a competitive programmer, security researcher and builder who focuses on where algorithmic logic meets real-world threat models. He works across AI security and DevOps, and runs CyberTutor, a cybersecurity education platform that reaches thousands of learners. LinkedIn YouTube GitHub 1st at Brunel in UKIEPC (ICPC) Top 70 in the UK, UKIEPC regional 200+ largest audience addressed 8+ hackathons and tech events 100K+ CyberTutor views"}, {"t": "About the Speaker › Leadership & Community", "u": "speaker/index.html#leadership-community", "x": "Beyond technology, Mohammed cares about leadership, communication and community. He is the President of the Brunel Cybersecurity Society (BCSS) and a public speaker representing Brunel University London, having addressed audiences of more than 200 people . He has taken part in 8+ hackathons and technology events , working with others on challenges across technology, business and social impact. Brunel Cybersecurity Society Founder and President, building a student community around practical security skills. Public speaking Representing Brunel at events, including this guest talk for OWASP Unive"}, {"t": "About the Speaker › Educational Platforms & Resources", "u": "speaker/index.html#educational-platforms-resources", "x": "Much of Mohammed's work outside engineering goes into CyberTutor , a cybersecurity education platform that spans a YouTube channel, a newsletter, a published book and productivity templates. YOUTUBE CyberTutor Practical, beginner-friendly walkthroughs on cybersecurity, ethical hacking, threat intelligence and cloud security. Learn, practise, protect. Watch on YouTube SUBSTACK Cyber News Breach case studies, news from the cyber world, and long-form articles on breaking into cybersecurity. Read Cyber News PUBLISHED BOOK The Three Corners Brain · Body · Soul : a practical guide to building balanc"}, {"t": "About the Speaker › Profiles", "u": "speaker/index.html#profiles", "x": "LinkedIn: Mohammed Zuoriki GitHub: Code-tech77 YouTube: CyberTutor Substack: Cyber News Follow on GitHub and subscribe on YouTube for upcoming builds, tools, and cloud and security projects. Questions and collaboration invitations are always welcome."}, {"t": "About the Speaker › Speaking", "u": "speaker/index.html#speaking", "x": "Event Talk Role Date OWASP University of Exeter Student Chapter Hacking AI: The Security Risks Behind AI Guest Talk Speaker October 2026"}, {"t": "Resources", "u": "resources/index.html", "x": "Resources Where to go next after the talk."}, {"t": "Resources › OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications", "u": "resources/index.html#owasp-top-10-for-llm-applications", "x": "The OWASP GenAI Security Project maintains a Top 10 list of the most critical risks for LLM applications. Here is how it maps to the five threat families from the talk: ID Risk Covered in the talk under LLM01 Prompt Injection Prompt Injection , Jailbreaks LLM02 Sensitive Information Disclosure Data Leaks LLM03 Supply Chain Poisoned Data LLM04 Data and Model Poisoning Poisoned Data LLM05 Improper Output Handling Insecure Actions LLM06 Excessive Agency Insecure Actions LLM07 System Prompt Leakage Data Leaks LLM08 Vector and Embedding Weaknesses Poisoned Data LLM09 Misinformation LLM10 Unbounded "}, {"t": "Resources › Practise safely", "u": "resources/index.html#practise-safely", "x": "Only test systems you own or have explicit permission to test. These sites are built for learning: Gandalf by Lakera: a level-based prompt-injection game. Try to get the model to reveal a password. gandalf.lakera.ai PortSwigger Web Security Academy, Web LLM attacks: free hands-on labs on LLM API abuse and indirect prompt injection. portswigger.net/web-security/llm-attacks OWASP Juice Shop: OWASP 's flagship deliberately vulnerable web app, a good foundation in classic web security. owasp.org/www-project-juice-shop"}, {"t": "Resources › Further reading", "u": "resources/index.html#further-reading", "x": "OWASP GenAI Security Project: guides, threat models and the LLM Top 10. MITRE ATLAS: a knowledge base of adversary tactics and techniques against AI systems. atlas.mitre.org UK NCSC: Guidelines for secure AI system development . ncsc.gov.uk"}, {"t": "Resources › Get involved", "u": "resources/index.html#get-involved", "x": "OWASP University of Exeter Student Chapter: come to the next session and bring a friend. CyberTutor: follow Mohammed's cybersecurity and AI content on YouTube and Substack ."}];
